// Science Break: 200 one-line lessons in mathematics and physics, shown one at a time every ten minutes of play (see ui_facts.cpp).
// Each line stands alone: it names the idea, says it in plain words, explains every symbol it uses and gives one concrete number.
#include "facts.h"

const ScienceFact SCIENCE_FACTS[SCIENCE_FACT_COUNT] = {
    // ---- numbers
    { "Zero", "Adding zero changes nothing (7 + 0 = 7) and multiplying by it gives zero (7 × 0 = 0), but dividing by zero has no answer at all: no number times 0 can ever make 7." },
    { "Fractions", "3/4 means: cut something into 4 equal parts and take 3. Fractions can only be added once the parts are the same size: 1/2 + 1/3 = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6." },
    { "Percent", "Percent means 'out of 100', so 25% = 25/100 = 0.25. A price that rises 50% and then falls 50% ends lower than it began (100 → 150 → 75), because the fall is taken from the bigger number." },
    { "Prime numbers", "A prime is a whole number above 1 that only 1 and itself divide evenly: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13... Every whole number above 1 is made of primes multiplied in exactly one way (60 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 5): primes are the atoms of multiplication." },
    { "Primes never run out", "Euclid's proof: multiply any list of primes and add 1 (2 × 3 × 5 + 1 = 31). Dividing that by any prime on the list leaves remainder 1, so a prime not on the list must divide it. No list of primes is ever complete." },
    { "Powers", "A power is repeated multiplication: 2⁵ ('two to the fifth') = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32. A sheet of paper folded in half 42 times would be 2⁴² sheets thick: farther than the distance to the Moon." },
    { "Scientific notation", "Huge and tiny numbers are written as a number times a power of ten: 3 × 10⁸ is 3 followed by 8 zeros (300,000,000, light's speed in metres per second), while 10⁻³ means 1/1000." },
    { "Square root", "The square root (√) of a number is what you multiply by itself to get it: √49 = 7 because 7 × 7 = 49. √2 = 1.41421356... never ends and never repeats a pattern." },
    { "Irrational numbers", "Why √2 is no fraction: if it were a/b in lowest terms, then a × a = 2 × b × b, so a is even, which forces b to be even too: not lowest terms after all. Numbers that are no fraction are called irrational." },
    { "Pi", "Pi (π ≈ 3.14159) is how many times a circle's width fits around its edge, for every circle there is. Its digits never end or repeat, yet 40 of them measure the visible universe to within the width of an atom." },
    { "0.999... equals 1", "0.999... (nines forever) is exactly 1: one third is 0.333..., three thirds are 0.999..., and three thirds make 1. One number can have two decimal spellings." },
    { "Binary", "Computers count with only 0 and 1, each place worth double the one to its right: 1101 in binary is 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = 13. Eight such digits (a byte) can hold 2⁸ = 256 different values." },

    // ---- algebra
    { "Variable", "A letter like x stands for a number not known yet. In 3x + 2 = 11 (3x means 3 times x) the only number that works is x = 3, since 3 × 3 + 2 = 11." },
    { "Order of operations", "2 + 3 × 4 is 14, not 20: multiplying comes before adding. Brackets come first of all: (2 + 3) × 4 = 20. Powers come before multiplying: 2 × 3² = 2 × 9 = 18." },
    { "Function", "A function is a machine with exactly one output for each input. f(x) = x² (x times x) turns 3 into 9 and also -3 into 9: two inputs may share an output, but one input never gives two." },
    { "Straight lines", "y = 2x + 1 draws a straight line: the 2 is its slope (one step right, two steps up) and the 1 is where it crosses the upright axis. Every equation y = m·x + c is a straight line." },
    { "Slope", "Slope = rise ÷ run: a road climbing 10 m over 100 m has slope 0.1, a 10% grade. It is the one number that says how steep a straight line is, and calculus stretches the idea to curves." },
    { "Solving by factoring", "x² - 5x + 6 = 0 (x² is x times x) splits into (x - 2)(x - 3) = 0. A product is zero only when one of its factors is zero, so the answers are x = 2 and x = 3." },
    { "Quadratic formula", "Any a·x² + b·x + c = 0 (a, b, c known numbers, x² = x times x) is solved by x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) ÷ 2a. The ± means: work it once with + and once with -, giving up to two answers." },
    { "Discriminant", "In the quadratic formula the part under the square root, b² - 4ac, decides everything: positive gives two answers, zero gives one, negative gives none among ordinary numbers, since no ordinary number times itself is negative." },
    { "Exponential growth", "Doubling grows faster than intuition: one grain of rice on a chessboard's first square, doubled on each next square, puts 2⁶³ ≈ 9 billion billion grains on the 64th: centuries of the whole world's rice harvest." },
    { "Logarithm", "A logarithm asks 'which power?': log₂(8) = 3 because 2³ = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8. Logarithms turn multiplying into adding: log(a × b) = log(a) + log(b), which is how slide rules multiplied." },
    { "Logarithmic scales", "Each step on a log scale multiplies: a magnitude 7 earthquake shakes the ground 10 times harder than a 6 and releases about 32 times the energy. Sound works the same: +10 decibels is ten times the power." },
    { "Gauss's sum", "1 + 2 + ... + 100: pair the ends (1+100, 2+99, ...) to get 50 pairs of 101, so 5050. In general 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1) ÷ 2, a trick the child Gauss is said to have found." },
    { "Infinite sums", "1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 + ... with no end adds up to exactly 1: each term covers half of the gap still left. An endless list of numbers can have a finite total." },
    { "Imaginary numbers", "No ordinary number times itself gives -1, so mathematicians added i with i × i = -1. Numbers like 3 + 2i live on a plane instead of a line, and multiplying by i turns any point a quarter turn around zero." },
    { "Two equations, two unknowns", "x + y = 10 and x - y = 2: add the two equations and y cancels, 2x = 12, so x = 6 and y = 4. Each equation is a line on a graph; the answer is where the two lines cross." },

    // ---- geometry and trigonometry
    { "Angles of a triangle", "A full turn is 360 degrees (°) and a square corner is 90°. The three angles of any flat triangle add to 180°: tear off the corners and they fit together into a straight line." },
    { "Pythagoras' theorem", "In a triangle with a square corner, the short sides a and b and the long side c obey a² + b² = c² (each side times itself). Sides 3 and 4 give a long side of 5, since 9 + 16 = 25." },
    { "Area of a circle", "A circle of radius r (centre to edge) has area π × r × r (π ≈ 3.14). Area grows with the radius times itself, so one 30 cm pizza holds more than two 20 cm pizzas together." },
    { "The rope round the Earth", "A circle's edge is 2 × π × radius (π ≈ 3.14). Tie a rope tight round the equator, then add just 6.3 m: it now floats 1 m above the ground the whole way round, because each 1 m of radius adds only 6.28 m of edge." },
    { "Square-cube law", "Scale a shape up 2 times and its area grows 4 times but its volume (and weight) 8 times. A giant ant would collapse: its weight would outgrow the strength of its legs, which grows only with their area." },
    { "Sphere", "A ball of radius r holds 4/3 × π × r³ of space (r³ = r × r × r, π ≈ 3.14) and has surface 4 × π × r², exactly four times a flat circle of the same radius. Double the radius: eight times the volume." },
    { "Radian", "A radian is the angle at which the arc along a circle is as long as its radius. A full turn is 2π radians (about 6.28), so 180° = π radians. Physics measures turning in radians because its formulas come out simplest." },
    { "Sine and cosine", "In a triangle with a square corner, an angle's sine = opposite side ÷ longest side, and its cosine = side next to it ÷ longest side. For 30° the sine is exactly 1/2: the side opposite is half the longest side." },
    { "The unit circle", "Walk round a circle of radius 1: at angle A you stand at (cos A, sin A), cosine across and sine up. Pythagoras on that point gives cos²A + sin²A = 1 for every angle there is." },
    { "Law of cosines", "Pythagoras for any triangle: c² = a² + b² - 2ab·cos C, where C is the angle between sides a and b (x² = x times x). At C = 90° the cosine is 0 and it becomes Pythagoras again." },
    { "Coordinates", "Descartes' idea: name every point by two numbers (across, up). Then shapes become equations, x² + y² = 25 is a circle of radius 5, and geometry can be done with algebra." },
    { "Tiling", "Only three regular shapes tile a floor on their own: triangles, squares and hexagons, because their corners (60°, 90°, 120°) divide 360° exactly. Bees build hexagons: the least wall for the most room." },
    { "Euler's polyhedron rule", "For any solid with flat faces and no holes: corners - edges + faces = 2. A cube has 8 corners, 12 edges and 6 faces: 8 - 12 + 6 = 2. A pyramid: 5 - 8 + 5 = 2." },
    { "Curved geometry", "On a globe, start at the North Pole, go down to the equator, a quarter of the way along it, and back up: a triangle with three 90° corners, 270° in all. The 180° rule only holds on flat surfaces." },

    // ---- calculus
    { "Limit", "A limit is where values are heading, even if they never arrive: 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, ... get as close to 0 as you like, so their limit is 0. All of calculus is built on limits." },
    { "Derivative", "The derivative is a curve's slope at one point: take a second point a tiny step h away, divide the rise by h, and let h shrink toward 0. For y = x² (x times x) the slope is 2x: at x = 3 it climbs 6 per step." },
    { "Finding the best", "At the top of a hill or the bottom of a valley a smooth curve is flat for an instant: its slope is 0. So to find a best value, set the derivative to zero, solve, and check it is really a top or a bottom." },
    { "Best rectangle", "40 m of fence round a rectangle: sides x and 20 - x give area x(20 - x). Its slope, 20 - 2x, is zero at x = 10, so the square (100 square metres) beats every other rectangle." },
    { "Integral", "An integral adds up infinitely many thin slices: the area under a curve is the sum of narrow rectangles (height × width) as the width shrinks toward 0. Distance travelled is the integral of speed over time." },
    { "Fundamental theorem of calculus", "Adding up all the tiny changes of something gives its total change. So areas (integrals) are found by undoing slopes (derivatives): the two halves of calculus are one idea seen from both ends." },
    { "Chain rule", "If A changes 3 times as fast as B, and B 2 times as fast as C, then A changes 3 × 2 = 6 times as fast as C. For a function inside a function, multiply the rates of each layer." },
    { "The number e", "e ≈ 2.71828: $1 at 100% yearly interest, added in ever smaller pieces all year long, grows to $e, not $2. The curve y = eˣ (e to the power x) is its own derivative: its slope always equals its height." },
    { "Differential equation", "A differential equation links a quantity to its own rate of change. 'Grows at a rate k times its size' has the answer y = y₀·eᵏᵗ (y₀ = start amount, t = time): bacteria, interest, chain reactions." },
    { "Taylor series", "Near a point, a smooth curve is copied ever better by adding terms: a line, then a parabola, then a cubic... eˣ = 1 + x + x²/2 + x³/6 + x⁴/24 + ...: this is roughly how calculators compute it." },
    { "Partial derivative", "When something depends on several inputs (a hill's height on east and north), a partial derivative is the slope in one direction while the other is held still." },
    { "Gradient", "The gradient is an arrow made of all the partial slopes: it points straight uphill and its length says how steep. Machine learning trains models by stepping the other way, downhill on the error (gradient descent)." },
    { "Zeno's paradox", "To cross a room you first cross half, then half of what is left, forever. You still arrive: infinitely many shrinking times, 1/2 + 1/4 + 1/8 + ... seconds, add up to a finite 1 second." },
    { "Divergence and curl", "For a flow like wind or water: divergence measures how much more leaves a tiny box than enters (a tap is a source), curl measures how much it spins there (a whirlpool). Together they describe any flow." },

    // ---- probability and statistics
    { "Probability", "Probability is a number from 0 (impossible) to 1 (certain). A fair die shows 6 with probability 1/6: one wanted outcome out of six equally likely ones." },
    { "Independent events", "When one event doesn't affect another, multiply their chances: two sixes in a row is 1/6 × 1/6 = 1/36. Ten heads in a row: (1/2) multiplied ten times = 1/1024." },
    { "Gambler's fallacy", "After 5 reds in a row, black is not 'due'. A fair wheel has no memory, so red is exactly as likely as ever. Luck doesn't even out by correcting itself; it only gets diluted by more spins." },
    { "Birthday paradox", "In a room of just 23 people, two probably share a birthday (a 50.7% chance). It's not 23 chances but 253: every pair of people is a chance for a match." },
    { "Expected value", "Expected value is the long-run average per try: a bet paying 10 with chance 1/10 and nothing otherwise is worth 1 per play. A lottery ticket's expected value is below its price: that's how lotteries pay for themselves." },
    { "The 99% test", "A test is 99% accurate for a disease 1 in 1000 people have. Test 1000 people: about 1 sick person tests positive and about 10 healthy ones do too. So a positive result means only about a 1 in 11 chance of being sick." },
    { "Bayes' theorem", "P(A|B) = P(B|A) × P(A) ÷ P(B), where P(A|B) reads 'the chance of A once B is known'. In words: evidence should raise your belief in A as much as it is more likely when A is true than in general." },
    { "Mean and median", "Mean: add everything, divide by the count. Median: the middle value when sorted. Nine people earn 30,000 and one earns 10 million: the mean is over a million, the median still 30,000. Medians resist extremes." },
    { "Standard deviation", "Standard deviation measures spread: the typical distance of values from their average. 49, 50, 51 and 0, 50, 100 both average 50, but the second set's standard deviation is about 50 times larger." },
    { "Bell curve", "Many measurements (heights, errors) pile up in a bell-shaped curve, the normal distribution. About 68% of values lie within one standard deviation of the average, 95% within two, 99.7% within three." },
    { "Law of large numbers", "Flip a coin 10 times and 7 heads is ordinary; flip it 10,000 times and the share of heads will almost surely be very close to 50%. Averages settle down as the number of trials grows." },
    { "Central limit theorem", "Add up many small independent random effects and the total follows the bell curve, whatever shape each single effect has. That's why the bell curve turns up nearly everywhere." },
    { "Correlation is not causation", "Ice cream sales and drownings rise together, yet neither causes the other: hot weather drives both. Only an experiment that changes one thing while holding the rest still shows a cause." },
    { "Monty Hall problem", "A prize is behind 1 of 3 doors. You pick one; the host, who knows, opens another empty door. Switching wins 2/3 of the time: your first pick was right only 1/3 of the time, and that never changed." },
    { "Regression to the mean", "A record-breaking season is usually followed by a weaker one, not because of a curse: an extreme result is partly luck, and luck doesn't repeat on demand. Extreme results drift back toward average." },
    { "Simpson's paradox", "A treatment can do better in every group and yet worse overall, if it was mostly given to the harder cases. Before trusting a combined average, ask how the groups were split." },
    { "Information in bits", "Information can be measured: a fair coin flip gives 1 bit (one yes-or-no answer). Picking one of 8 equal options gives 3 bits, since 2 × 2 × 2 = 8. Surprising news carries more bits than predictable news." },

    // ---- logic, counting and computing
    { "If-then logic", "'If it rains, the street is wet' says nothing about dry days. A wet street doesn't prove rain (a hose could do it); believing it does is the error called affirming the consequent." },
    { "Contrapositive", "'If P then Q' is exactly as true as 'if not Q, then not P'. 'Every dog is a mammal' means 'anything that isn't a mammal isn't a dog'. Prove one and you have proved the other." },
    { "Proof by induction", "Like dominoes: show the first one falls, and that any falling domino knocks over the next. Then they all fall, so a statement is proved for every whole number 1, 2, 3, ... at once." },
    { "Factorial", "n! ('n factorial') = 1 × 2 × ... × n, the number of ways to put n things in order. A deck can be shuffled 52! ≈ 8 × 10⁶⁷ ways, so a well-shuffled deck is almost surely in an order never seen before." },
    { "Combinations", "Choosing 2 of 5 people for a team gives 10 ways: 5 × 4 ordered picks, halved because A-then-B is the same team as B-then-A. In general, k from n: n! ÷ (k! × (n - k)!), where n! = 1 × 2 × ... × n." },
    { "Pigeonhole principle", "Put 13 people in a room and two must share a birth month: 13 people can't fill 12 months with one each. Obvious, yet it proves surprising things." },
    { "Graphs", "In maths a graph is dots joined by lines: cities and roads, friends, web pages and links. Euler founded the subject by proving no walk crosses all seven bridges of Königsberg exactly once." },
    { "Fibonacci numbers", "Each is the sum of the two before: 0, 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21... Neighbours' ratio approaches 1.618..., the golden ratio, and the spirals in a sunflower head often come in Fibonacci counts." },
    { "Clock arithmetic", "9 o'clock plus 5 hours is 2 o'clock: counting wraps round at 12. Mathematicians write 14 ≡ 2 (mod 12): same remainder after dividing by 12. Card payments and internet encryption run on this wrap-around maths." },
    { "Big O", "Big O says how work grows with size: comparing every pair of n items takes about n² steps (n × n), so 10 times the items means 100 times the work. Good sorting takes only about n × log n." },
    { "Binary search", "To find a name in a sorted list of a million, check the middle and throw away the wrong half; repeat. About 20 checks suffice, because 2²⁰ (2 multiplied 20 times) is just over a million." },
    { "Boolean logic", "Boolean logic has only true and false, combined by AND (both true), OR (at least one true) and NOT (flip). Every computer is billions of tiny switches combining these three operations." },
    { "Halting problem", "Turing proved no program can decide, for every program and input, whether it will ever stop: a checker fed a twisted copy of itself would contradict its own answer. Some questions cannot be computed at all." },
    { "Gödel's incompleteness", "Any consistent set of rules strong enough for ordinary arithmetic contains statements it can neither prove nor disprove. No single fixed rulebook can capture all of mathematics." },
    { "Public-key encryption", "Multiplying two huge prime numbers is quick; splitting the product back into them is so slow that no known method finishes in a lifetime for numbers hundreds of digits long. Internet encryption (RSA) rests on this one-way street." },
    { "Nash equilibrium", "A Nash equilibrium is a set of choices where nobody gains by changing only their own. In the prisoner's dilemma both betray, though both staying silent would serve each better: individual logic can lead a group astray." },

    // ---- vectors and matrices
    { "Vector", "A vector is an arrow, a size with a direction, written as a list of numbers: (3, 4) means 3 across and 4 up, and its length is 5 by Pythagoras. Velocity, force and wind are vectors." },
    { "Adding vectors", "Vectors add tip to tail: walk (3, 0) then (0, 4) and you end at (3, 4), 5 from the start. A plane flying north in a wind blowing east travels north-east over the ground." },
    { "Dot product", "Multiply matching numbers and add: (1, 2)·(3, 4) = 1×3 + 2×4 = 11. It also equals length × length × cosine of the angle between the arrows, so it is 0 exactly when they are at right angles." },
    { "Matrix", "A matrix is a grid of numbers that moves arrows: it can rotate, stretch or shear all of space at once. Every 3D game redraws its world by multiplying millions of points by matrices every frame." },
    { "Determinant", "A matrix's determinant says how it scales areas: 2 doubles them, 0 squashes space flat (and then it can't be undone), and a negative value means space was flipped like a mirror image." },
    { "Eigenvector", "An eigenvector of a transformation is an arrow it only stretches or shrinks, never turns; the stretch factor is its eigenvalue. Google's first ranking of web pages was an eigenvector of the web's link matrix." },
    { "Dimension", "The dimension of a space is how many independent directions it has: a line 1, a sheet 2, a room 3. Independent means none of them can be built by combining the others." },
    { "Singular value decomposition", "Any matrix is a turn, then a stretch along perpendicular axes, then another turn. Keeping only the biggest stretches compresses images and finds the strongest patterns hidden in data." },

    // ---- infinity, shape and structure
    { "Comparing infinities", "There are as many even numbers as whole numbers: pair 1 with 2, 2 with 4, 3 with 6, ... forever, nothing left over. Cantor showed infinite sets can be compared by such pairings." },
    { "Cantor's diagonal", "List any endless set of decimals between 0 and 1. Build a new one differing from the 1st in its 1st digit, the 2nd in its 2nd, and so on: it's on no list. So there are more decimals than whole numbers." },
    { "Hilbert's Hotel", "A hotel with infinitely many rooms is full. A guest arrives: everyone moves from room n to room n + 1, and room 1 is free. With infinity, 'full' doesn't mean 'no room'." },
    { "Euler's identity", "e^(iπ) + 1 = 0 joins five key numbers (e ≈ 2.718, i with i × i = -1, π ≈ 3.14, 1 and 0). It says: start at 1 and turn half a circle round zero on the plane of numbers, and you land on -1." },
    { "Topology", "Topology studies shapes you may stretch but not cut or glue. To a topologist a coffee mug and a doughnut are the same: each has exactly one hole and can be squeezed into the other." },
    { "Fourier's discovery", "Any repeating signal, a violin note or a heartbeat, is a sum of simple waves of different frequencies. MP3 and JPEG files shrink by keeping the important waves and dropping the rest." },
    { "Fractals", "A fractal repeats its pattern at every zoom. A coastline's measured length keeps growing as your ruler shrinks; its fractal dimension (Britain's about 1.25) sits between a line (1) and a surface (2)." },
    { "Möbius strip", "Give a paper strip a half twist and join the ends: it has one side and one edge. Draw a line down the middle and you come back on the 'other' side without lifting the pen." },
    { "Four colour theorem", "Four colours always suffice to colour any flat map so that neighbouring countries differ. In 1976 it became the first major theorem proved with a computer checking cases too many for humans." },
    { "Goldbach's conjecture", "Every even number above 2 seems to be the sum of two primes (10 = 3 + 7, 100 = 47 + 53). It has been checked up to 4 × 10¹⁸ and never proved: simple questions can be the hardest." },
    { "Benford's law", "In many real data sets (populations, prices, river lengths) about 30% of numbers start with the digit 1 and under 5% with 9. Fraud checks use it: invented numbers use each first digit too evenly." },

    // ---- motion and forces
    { "Acceleration", "Acceleration is how fast velocity changes: 0 to 100 km/h in 10 s gains 10 km/h every second. Turning counts too: on a bend you accelerate sideways even at a steady speed." },
    { "Newton's first law", "A moving object keeps moving straight at the same speed until a force acts on it. Things on Earth slow down because friction and air push on them, not because motion fades by itself." },
    { "Newton's second law", "Force = mass × acceleration (F = m·a). The same push speeds a light cart up a lot and a heavy truck a little. 1 newton is the force that speeds 1 kg up by 1 metre per second every second." },
    { "Newton's third law", "Push on anything and it pushes back on you just as hard, the opposite way. A rocket throws gas backwards and the gas throws the rocket forwards: it needs no air to push against." },
    { "All things fall alike", "Without air, a hammer and a feather fall together, as an astronaut showed on the Moon in 1971. Gravity pulls a heavier thing harder, but it is harder to speed up by exactly the same factor." },
    { "Free fall", "Near Earth, falling things gain about 9.8 m/s of speed every second (called g). After 3 s, ignoring air, a stone moves at about 29 m/s and has fallen 44 m: distance = half × g × time × time." },
    { "Projectiles", "A bullet fired level and one dropped from the same height hit the ground together: falling and sideways motion don't affect each other. Without air, a ball thrown at 45° goes farthest." },
    { "Momentum", "Momentum = mass × velocity, and in a crash the total before equals the total after. A 1-tonne car at 20 m/s hitting a parked 1-tonne car, if they lock together, moves off at 10 m/s." },
    { "Kinetic energy", "Energy of motion = half × mass × speed² (speed times itself). Double the speed, four times the energy: from 100 km/h a car needs about four times the braking distance it needs from 50." },
    { "Conservation of energy", "Energy is never made or destroyed, only changed in form: a falling ball's height energy becomes speed, then heat and sound when it lands. That's why perpetual motion machines can't exist." },
    { "Going in circles", "Moving in a circle needs a pull toward the centre (a string, gravity, a tyre's grip) giving acceleration = speed² ÷ radius. Double your speed through a bend and you need four times the grip." },
    { "Newton's gravity", "Two masses attract with force G × m₁ × m₂ ÷ r², where m₁ and m₂ are the masses, r their distance and G a tiny fixed number. Twice as far apart, the pull is four times weaker." },
    { "Orbit", "An orbit is endless falling: a satellite falls toward Earth but moves sideways so fast (about 7.8 km/s up low) that the ground curves away beneath it as fast as it falls." },
    { "Weightless in orbit", "On the space station gravity is still about 90% as strong as on the ground. Astronauts float because they and the station fall around Earth together." },
    { "Torque", "Torque is turning force: force × distance from the pivot. That's why door handles sit far from the hinges and a longer wrench frees a stuck bolt: twice the length, twice the turn for the same push." },
    { "Angular momentum", "A spinning skater who pulls in their arms spins faster: spin times spread (angular momentum) stays the same. A collapsing star does the same and becomes a pulsar turning hundreds of times a second." },
    { "Hooke's law", "A spring pulls back in proportion to its stretch: force = -k × stretch (k is its stiffness; the minus means 'backwards'). So a weight on it bounces at a steady rhythm: the heart of clocks and of vibration everywhere." },
    { "Pendulum", "A pendulum's swing time depends only on its length and gravity, not its weight or (for small swings) its width: time = 2π√(length ÷ g), with g ≈ 9.8. A 1 m pendulum swings there and back in about 2 s." },

    // ---- waves and light
    { "Waves", "A wave carries energy, not matter: in a stadium wave each fan only stands up and sits down. Wave speed = frequency × wavelength (crests per second × distance between crests)." },
    { "Thunder distance", "Sound travels about 343 m per second in air, light almost a million times faster. Count the seconds between lightning and thunder and divide by 3: that's roughly the distance in kilometres." },
    { "Interference", "Overlapping waves add: crest on crest makes a bigger crest, crest on trough cancels out. Noise-cancelling headphones play an upside-down copy of the noise so the two cancel." },
    { "Standing waves", "A string fixed at both ends can only vibrate in whole numbers of half-waves. So it sounds one note plus a set of higher ones (harmonics) whose mix gives each instrument its own voice." },
    { "Refraction", "Light slows down in water and glass, and when it enters at a slant it bends. That's why a straw looks broken in a glass of water, and how lenses in eyes and cameras focus light." },
    { "Rainbows", "White light is every colour mixed. Water and glass bend each colour by a slightly different amount (violet most, red least), so a raindrop or a prism fans them out into a rainbow." },
    { "Doppler effect", "A siren sounds higher coming toward you and lower going away: its waves bunch up in front and stretch out behind. The same shift in starlight tells how fast stars move toward or away from us." },
    { "Light is a wave of fields", "Radio, microwaves, infrared, visible light, ultraviolet, X-rays and gamma rays are all the same thing, electromagnetic waves, differing only in wavelength: from kilometres down to less than an atom." },
    { "Photon energy", "Light comes in packets (photons), each with energy = h × frequency, where h is a tiny fixed number (Planck's constant). Ultraviolet burns skin while brighter red light can't: each UV packet carries more energy." },
    { "Blue sky", "Air scatters short blue waves several times more than long red ones, so blue light reaches your eye from every part of the sky. At sunset the light crosses so much air that the blue is scattered away and red remains." },

    // ---- heat
    { "Temperature", "Temperature measures how fast the atoms of a thing jiggle on average. Heat flows by itself only from hotter to colder, which is why coffee cools on the table and never warms up." },
    { "Absolute zero", "-273.15 °C (0 kelvin) is the coldest possible temperature: atoms have the least motion physics allows. The kelvin scale starts there, so water freezes at 273.15 K." },
    { "Heat capacity", "Warming 1 kg of water by 1 degree takes about 4200 joules, far more than most materials need. That's why seas keep coastal towns mild and a pot of water takes so long to boil." },
    { "Cold metal", "Metal and wood in the same room are the same temperature, yet metal feels colder: it carries heat away from your hand much faster. Your skin senses heat flow, not temperature." },
    { "Latent heat", "Boiling water stays at 100 °C (at sea level) however hard you heat it: the extra energy goes into pulling molecules apart into steam, not into raising the temperature." },
    { "Gas law", "Pressure × volume = amount of gas × R × temperature (R a fixed number, temperature counted from absolute zero). Heat a sealed can and its pressure climbs; squeeze air in a bike pump and it heats up." },
    { "Entropy", "The total disorder (entropy) of a closed system never decreases. Eggs break but never unbreak and ice melts in a warm drink: there are vastly more messy arrangements than tidy ones." },
    { "Boltzmann's entropy", "S = k × log W: entropy S counts W, the number of ways the atoms could be arranged and still look the same from outside (k is a tiny fixed constant). More possible arrangements, more entropy." },
    { "Carnot limit", "No heat engine turns all its heat into work. Its best possible efficiency is 1 - T(cold) ÷ T(hot), temperatures in kelvin: between 800 K and 300 K no engine can beat 62.5%." },
    { "Glowing heat", "Every warm thing glows, mostly in invisible infrared. The power it radiates grows with the fourth power of its absolute temperature: double the temperature, 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 16 times the glow." },

    // ---- electricity and magnetism
    { "Electric charge", "Charge comes in two kinds, plus and minus: like charges repel, opposite charges attract. Rub a balloon on your hair and electrons move onto the balloon, which then clings to a wall." },
    { "Electric force", "Electric force, like gravity, weakens with distance squared, but it is enormously stronger: two electrons push apart about 10⁴² times harder than gravity pulls them together." },
    { "Ohm's law", "Picture a circuit as water pipes: voltage is the pressure, current is how much flows per second, resistance is how narrow the pipe is. Ohm's law: voltage = current × resistance." },
    { "Electrical power", "Power = voltage × current. A 2000-watt kettle on 230 volts draws almost 9 amperes. The kilowatt-hour on your bill is 1000 watts for one hour: 3.6 million joules." },
    { "Electromagnet", "Every electric current makes a magnetic field round it (Oersted, 1820). Coil the wire and you have an electromagnet: this link between electricity and magnetism drives every electric motor." },
    { "Induction", "Faraday's law: a changing magnetic field creates a voltage. Spin a magnet inside a coil and current flows: almost every power station makes its electricity this way." },
    { "Magnetic force", "A charge moving through a magnetic field is pushed sideways, at right angles to both its motion and the field. That push turns motors and steers particles from the Sun to the poles, lighting the auroras." },
    { "Maxwell and light", "Maxwell found changing electric fields make magnetic fields and the reverse, so the two can carry each other through empty space as a wave. He worked out its speed: exactly light's. Light is that wave." },
    { "Faraday cage", "Inside a closed metal shell, fields from outside charges vanish: charges in the metal rearrange to cancel them. That's why you're safe in a car struck by lightning and phones lose signal in lifts." },

    // ---- relativity
    { "Light's speed", "Light in empty space moves at 299,792 km per second, and everyone measures that same speed however fast they themselves move. Einstein built special relativity on this one strange fact." },
    { "Time dilation", "Moving clocks tick slower: at 87% of light's speed a clock runs at half the rate seen by someone at rest. Fast particles from space live longer than they 'should' for exactly this reason." },
    { "Lorentz factor", "γ = 1 ÷ √(1 - v²/c²), where v is your speed and c light's, says how much time stretches and lengths shrink. At everyday speeds it is 1.000000000...; at 99% of light speed it is about 7." },
    { "E = mc²", "Mass is a form of energy: E = m × c × c, with c light's speed, a huge multiplier. One gram of matter fully turned into energy gives about 90 trillion joules, as much as a 20-kiloton bomb." },
    { "Equivalence principle", "Einstein's 'happiest thought': someone falling freely feels no weight, so locally gravity and acceleration can't be told apart. From it he worked out that gravity is the bending of space and time." },
    { "Curved spacetime", "General relativity: matter tells spacetime how to curve, and curved spacetime tells matter how to move. Earth orbits the Sun by following the straightest possible path through that curve." },
    { "Gravity slows clocks", "Clocks tick slower deeper in gravity. GPS satellite clocks gain about 38 microseconds a day overall; uncorrected, positions would drift by kilometres within a single day." },
    { "Black holes", "A black hole is mass squeezed so tight that nothing, not even light, gets out. Its edge has radius 2GM ÷ c² (M its mass, G gravity's constant, c light's speed): squeezed that tight, Earth would be under 1 cm across." },
    { "Twin paradox", "A twin who flies to a star near light speed and back comes home younger than the one who stayed. It isn't symmetric: only the traveller turned around, and so only the traveller's clock lost time." },
    { "Gravitational waves", "Accelerating masses send ripples through spacetime. In 2015, 4 km detectors measured a stretch under a thousandth of a proton's width, from two black holes merging over a billion light years away." },

    // ---- quantum
    { "Quantised energy", "Inside an atom an electron can only have certain energies, like standing on stairs but never between steps. Dropping a step gives out light of one exact colour, each element's own fingerprint." },
    { "Double slit", "Fire electrons one at a time at two slits and the dots on the screen slowly build a striped pattern of interfering waves. Matter travels like a wave and arrives like a particle." },
    { "Wavefunction", "A quantum particle is described by a wavefunction ψ ('psi'); the size of ψ squared at a place gives the probability of finding the particle there. Quantum theory predicts chances, not certainties." },
    { "Superposition", "Until measured, a quantum system can be in a blend of states, say spin up and spin down at once. Measuring gives just one result, with chances set by how much of each is in the blend." },
    { "Uncertainty principle", "The more exactly a particle's position is pinned down, the less exactly its momentum (mass × velocity) can be, and the reverse. Not poor instruments: a short wave pulse simply has no single wavelength." },
    { "Matter waves", "Everything has a wavelength = h ÷ momentum (h is Planck's tiny constant, momentum = mass × speed). An electron's is about an atom wide, so electron microscopes see detail light can't; a thrown ball's is unimaginably small." },
    { "Tunnelling", "A particle can cross a barrier it hasn't the energy to climb, because its wave leaks through. The Sun shines thanks to it: protons tunnel close enough to fuse." },
    { "Entanglement", "Entangled particles share one joint state: measure one and you know its partner's result at once, however far away. Yet no message travels this way: each result on its own is random." },
    { "Pauli exclusion", "No two electrons can share exactly the same quantum state, so they stack into higher and higher shells round the nucleus. That gives atoms their size and chemistry, and solid matter its solidity." },
    { "Bell's theorem", "If particles carried hidden instructions fixed in advance, results of distant measurements would obey a certain limit. Experiments break that limit (Nobel prize, 2022): nature is stranger than hidden instructions." },
    { "Photoelectric effect", "Light knocks electrons out of metal only if its frequency is high enough, however bright it is. Einstein explained it with light packets (photons) and won his Nobel prize for that, not for relativity." },

    // ---- atoms and nuclei
    { "Inside an atom", "An atom is a tiny heavy nucleus (protons and neutrons) with electrons around it. Were the nucleus a marble at the centre of a stadium, the electrons would roam the stands: the rest is empty space." },
    { "Elements and isotopes", "The number of protons in the nucleus decides the element: 1 hydrogen, 6 carbon, 79 gold. Same protons, different neutrons gives isotopes of one element, like carbon-12 and carbon-14." },
    { "Half-life", "A half-life is the time for half of a radioactive sample to decay. Carbon-14's is about 5730 years, so a bone holding a quarter of its original carbon-14 died about 11,460 years ago." },
    { "Random yet exact", "No one can predict when a single unstable nucleus will decay, yet a large pile follows an exact curve: each half-life leaves half of what was there. Randomness in the small, precision in the large." },
    { "Mass defect", "A helium nucleus weighs about 0.7% less than the two protons and two neutrons it is made of. The missing mass became the energy binding it (E = mc²), and that is the energy the Sun gives off." },
    { "Fission", "A uranium nucleus struck by a neutron splits in two and frees 2 or 3 more neutrons, which can split more nuclei: a chain reaction. Controlled it runs a power plant; uncontrolled it is a bomb." },
    { "Fusion", "Light nuclei such as hydrogen merge into heavier ones such as helium, turning a little mass into much energy. It powers every star, but only at millions of degrees can nuclei be forced close enough." },
    { "The four forces", "Physics knows four basic forces: gravity, electromagnetism, the strong force (holds nuclei together) and the weak force (behind some radioactivity). Everything you've ever touched pushes back by electromagnetism." },

    // ---- fluids
    { "Archimedes' principle", "Anything in water is pushed up by a force equal to the weight of the water it moves aside. A steel ship floats because its hollow hull moves aside more water than the whole ship weighs." },
    { "Deep water", "Every 10 m of water depth adds about one atmosphere of pressure (the weight of all the air above us). At the ocean's deepest point, about 11 km down, it is over 1000 atmospheres." },
    { "Density", "Density = mass ÷ volume. Ice is about 9% less dense than water, so it floats and lakes freeze from the top down, the ice sheltering the water and life beneath it." },
    { "Bernoulli's principle", "Where a flowing fluid speeds up, its pressure drops. Squeeze a hose and the water speeds out; air rushing faster over a wing's curved top presses down less than air underneath presses up." },
    { "Reynolds number", "Speed × size ÷ (viscosity ÷ density), the Reynolds number, tells smooth flow from turbulent. A bacterium lives at about 0.0001: to it, water is thick as syrup and it stops dead the instant it stops swimming." },

    // ---- space
    { "Light-year", "A light-year is a distance, not a time: how far light travels in a year, about 9.5 trillion km. The nearest star past the Sun is 4.2 light-years away, so we see it as it was 4.2 years ago." },
    { "Escape velocity", "The launch speed to never fall back is √(2 × G × mass ÷ radius): about 11.2 km/s from Earth, only 2.4 km/s from the Moon. It is the same for a pebble and for a spaceship." },
    { "Kepler's third law", "A planet's year, multiplied by itself, grows with its distance from the Sun multiplied by itself three times. Mars is 1.52 times farther out than Earth, so its year lasts about 1.88 Earth years." },
    { "Expanding universe", "Distant galaxies race away from us faster the farther they are, not because we are the centre: space itself is stretching everywhere, like dots on a balloon being blown up." },
    { "Oldest light", "The whole sky glows faintly with microwaves at 2.7 degrees above absolute zero: light from when the universe, about 380,000 years old, first became transparent." },
    { "Tides", "The Moon pulls harder on Earth's near side than on its far side, stretching the oceans into two bulges. Earth turns under both, so most coasts get two high tides a day." },

    // ---- complex systems
    { "Chaos", "Some systems that follow exact rules blow up tiny differences until they dominate: a change in the sixth decimal of today's weather can change the forecast completely two weeks ahead." },
    { "Feedback", "Positive feedback amplifies (a microphone near its speaker squeals); negative feedback steadies (a thermostat turns the heating off when warm). Stable systems in nature and machines run on negative feedback." },
    { "Emergence", "A flock of birds turns as one with no leader: each bird only keeps close, avoids collisions and matches its neighbours' heading. Simple local rules can create complex wholes." },
    { "Noether's theorem", "Every symmetry of nature brings a conserved quantity: physics working the same today as tomorrow gives conservation of energy; the same here as there gives conservation of momentum." },
    { "Phase transition", "Cross a threshold and behaviour changes abruptly: water freezes at 0 °C, iron loses its magnetism above 770 °C. Near the threshold, tiny causes have huge effects." },
    { "Random walk", "Take n steps in random directions and you typically end about √n steps from the start, not n (√ = square root). That's why smells and heat spread fast at first and then ever more slowly." },
};
